From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:28:20 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vkr: let VKR_DEVICE_HEAP_MIB force the advertised device-local
 heap size

On a shared-memory guest the device-local heap vkr advertises is a fiction that
the guest's allocator nevertheless sizes itself from, and on the Xen RPi4 board
that killed the driver domain.

v3dv's compute_heap_size() reports a percentage of the host's *system* RAM as
device-local VRAM (645 MiB on a 1408 MiB DomD) and vkr forwards it verbatim.
The AAOS guest's Skia VMA then takes heapSize/8 as its block size, because any
heap of 1 GiB or less is a "small heap" to it (VMA_SMALL_HEAP_MAX_SIZE), and
only above that does it use the 4 MiB preferredLargeHeapBlockSize Skia
configures (external/skia VulkanAMDMemoryAllocator.cpp). Every one of those
blocks is a fully populated v3d BO in DomD, so DomD pays for the block and not
for what the guest uses of it: HWUI RenderThreads took such blocks by the
handful -- 96 MiB each, ~600 MiB per app, with DomD at 1664 MiB (named by a
diagnostic log of allocations >= 64 MiB) --
DomD went into a memory-reclaim livelock with both vCPUs in shrink_node /
__buffer_migrate_folio, qemu could no longer service the guest's ioreqs, and
the guest soft-locked on its virtio-gpu doorbell write until its watchdog killed
system_server. Raising DomD from 1408 to 1664 MiB grew the per-app burst from
~400 to ~600 MiB, which is what tied the burst to the advertised heap.

Clamping the advertised heap does not fix this, and that was measured too: at
512 MiB the guest is still below VMA_SMALL_HEAP_MAX_SIZE and simply used 64 MiB
blocks instead -- one RenderThread context took five in a row and DomD
livelocked again on the screen-transition benchmark. What bounds the guest is
advertising a heap ABOVE the threshold, which puts it on the path a desktop GPU
gets: 4 MiB blocks for small things and dedicated allocations of the real size
for images.

VKR_DEVICE_HEAP_MIB therefore forces the advertised device-local heap size to
exactly the given value (the Xen device model sets 1088 through doma.cfg
qemu_envs, the smallest round value above 1 GiB). It is inert when the env is
unset, so upstream and non-Xen users are unaffected, and it is ignored with a log
line for anything outside 1..65536. The size is a hint to the guest's allocator;
v3dv never enforces heap sizes. The override rewrites the cached properties, so a
vkGetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties2 query that chains
VkPhysicalDeviceMemoryBudgetPropertiesEXT still sees v3dv's own numbers.

With it, the cold-start stress, the screen-transition benchmarks and a ten-cycle
soak all pass with no guest allocation of 64 MiB or more and DomD MemAvailable
never below ~460 MiB.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/venus/vkr_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_physical_device.c
@@ -3,6 +3,9 @@
  * SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
  */
 
+#include <errno.h>
+#include <inttypes.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include "vkr_physical_device.h"
 
 #include "venus-protocol/vn_protocol_renderer_device.h"
@@ -171,6 +174,62 @@ vkr_physical_device_init_memory_properties(struct vkr_physical_device *physical_
    VkPhysicalDevice handle = physical_dev->base.handle.physical_device;
    vk->GetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties(handle, &physical_dev->memory_properties);
 
+   /*
+    * On a shared-memory guest (Xen DomA on RPi4) v3dv reports a device-local heap
+    * sized as a percentage of DomD's system RAM (v3dv compute_heap_size(): 645 MiB
+    * on a 1408 MiB DomD) and vkr forwards that number to the guest. None of that
+    * "VRAM" is real -- it is DomD's memory -- and the guest's allocator sizes itself
+    * from the number: Skia's VMA (HWUI and SurfaceFlinger) takes heapSize/8 as its
+    * block size for any heap of 1 GiB or less (VMA_SMALL_HEAP_MAX_SIZE), and every
+    * block here is a fully populated v3d BO in DomD. Measured on RPi4 2026-09-12:
+    * HWUI RenderThreads took 96 MiB blocks by the handful (~600 MiB per app), DomD
+    * went into a memory-reclaim livelock with both vCPUs in shrink_node, the device
+    * model could then no longer service ioreqs and the guest soft-locked.
+    *
+    * Clamping the heap does not fix that: at 512 MiB the guest is still below
+    * VMA_SMALL_HEAP_MAX_SIZE and simply used 64 MiB blocks instead (DomD livelocked
+    * again on the screen-transition benchmark). What bounds the guest is advertising
+    * a heap ABOVE the threshold, which makes VMA switch to the 4 MiB
+    * preferredLargeHeapBlockSize Skia configures and turn large images into
+    * dedicated allocations of their real size.
+    *
+    * VKR_DEVICE_HEAP_MIB forces the advertised device-local heap to exactly its
+    * value, whatever the driver reports; the Xen device model sets 1088 through
+    * doma.cfg qemu_envs, the smallest round value above 1 GiB. Inert when the env is
+    * unset, so upstream and non-Xen users are unaffected. The size is a hint to the
+    * guest's allocator; v3dv never enforces heap sizes.
+    *
+    * Scope: EVERY heap with VK_MEMORY_HEAP_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT is forced to the same
+    * value (v3dv advertises one). The write lands in the cached properties, which is
+    * what vkGetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties and a pNext-less
+    * vkGetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties2 return; a query that chains
+    * VkPhysicalDeviceMemoryBudgetPropertiesEXT is forwarded straight to the driver and
+    * therefore still sees v3dv's own heap size and budget.
+    */
+   {
+      const char *heap_env = getenv("VKR_DEVICE_HEAP_MIB");
+      if (heap_env && heap_env[0]) {
+         char *end = NULL;
+         errno = 0;
+         unsigned long long mib = strtoull(heap_env, &end, 10);
+         if (errno || *end || mib == 0 || mib > 65536) {
+            vkr_log("ignoring VKR_DEVICE_HEAP_MIB=%s (expected 1..65536)", heap_env);
+         } else {
+            const uint64_t forced = (uint64_t)mib << 20;
+            VkPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties *mp = &physical_dev->memory_properties;
+            for (uint32_t i = 0; i < mp->memoryHeapCount; i++) {
+               if ((mp->memoryHeaps[i].flags & VK_MEMORY_HEAP_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT) &&
+                   mp->memoryHeaps[i].size != forced) {
+                  vkr_log("overriding device-local heap %u from %" PRIu64 " MiB to %" PRIu64 " MiB",
+                          i, (uint64_t)(mp->memoryHeaps[i].size >> 20),
+                          (uint64_t)(forced >> 20));
+                  mp->memoryHeaps[i].size = forced;
+               }
+            }
+         }
+      }
+   }
+
    /* XXX When a VkMemoryType has VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT, we
     * assume any VkDeviceMemory with the memory type can be made external and
     * be exportable.  That is incorrect but is what we have to live with with
